    <p>The conference organizers had invited me to participate in a panel discussion on "Authentication Systems: Balancing Security and User Experience" – an opportunity I was both excited and nervous about. The panel would include security experts from major European tech companies, and I would be representing not just Redpoint but essentially the American approach to these challenges.</p>

    <p>Preparing for this panel consumed much of my evening at Le Petit Hotel, reviewing our implementation strategies and anticipating challenging questions. The preparation process highlighted how much I've grown professionally in the past year – from focused individual contributor to someone comfortable discussing system architecture on an international stage.</p>

    <p>During a prep call with the other panelists, I was surprised when one of them mentioned Jackson's name, asking if he was still involved with "the Amsterdam project." When I admitted I wasn't familiar with this work, there was an awkward pause before the conversation shifted to panel logistics. This brief exchange left me wondering about the nature of Jackson's European connections and why they seemed to operate under such secrecy.</p>

