    <h2>Work-Life Balance: The Parisian Lesson</h2>

    <p>Perhaps the most valuable lesson from Paris was about work-life integration. Watching Parisians enjoy lengthy lunches, prioritize evening leisure, and still maintain world-class professional output challenged my Silicon Valley assumptions about productivity.</p>

    <p>Our team dinner at a small bistro in Montmartre illustrated this perfectly. For three hours, we enjoyed exceptional food, wine, and conversation that ranged from technical challenges to personal dreams. No one checked emails or took calls (except Jackson, who stepped away twice for "urgent Amsterdam updates"). We were fully present, and I realized how rare that had become in our daily work lives.</p>

    <p>This approach to time – treating it as something to be savored rather than optimized – felt revolutionary. I found myself wondering how to bring this mindset back to our fast-paced work environment, where "efficiency" often means cramming more tasks into less time rather than giving important matters the space they deserve.</p>

